  "status":"PROOF_CANDIDATE",
  "target":"For every nonnegative integer n and every nonempty compact E⊆R^n, with I=[0,1] and E×I carrying the metric induced by the Euclidean norm on R^(n+1), prove dim_H(E×I)=dim_H(E)+1. Hausdorff dimension uses the convention dim_H(∅)=0.",
  "proof":"Write D=dim_H(E) and F=E×[0,1]. All distances in the product are Euclidean concatenated-coordinate distances: d((x,t),(y,u))=(|x−y|²+|t−u|²)^(1/2). We use Hausdorff contents defined by infima of sums of powers of diameters over covers with diameters at most δ; the normalization constant in the definition of Hausdorff measure has no effect on dimension.\n\nFirst prove dim_H(F)≤D+1. Fix any real q>D. Since E is bounded and lies in finite-dimensional Euclidean space, D is finite. The definition of dimension gives H^q(E)=0. Consequently, for every sufficiently small δ>0 and every ε>0, there is a countable cover (U_j) of E with r_j=diam(U_j)≤δ and Σ_j r_j^q<ε. (If necessary replace each member by a slightly larger set to avoid zero-diameter covering members; the enlargement can be chosen with total q-cost arbitrarily small.) For each j with r_j>0, divide [0,1] into at most 1/r_j+1 intervals of length at most r_j. The products of U_j with these intervals cover U_j×[0,1], and each product has diameter at most √2 r_j in the specified Euclidean metric. Their total (q+1)-power cost is at most (1/r_j+1)(√2 r_j)^(q+1)≤2(√2)^(q+1)r_j^q when r_j≤1. Thus these products, over all j, cover F, have diameters tending uniformly to zero as δ→0, and total (q+1)-cost at most 2(√2)^(q+1)ε. Letting ε→0 proves H^(q+1)(F)=0 and hence dim_H(F)≤q+1. Let q decrease to D to obtain dim_H(F)≤D+1.\n\nFor the reverse inequality, first suppose D>0, and choose any real s with 0≤s<D. Then H^s(E)=∞ by the defining threshold property of Hausdorff dimension. Consider an arbitrary countable cover (V_i) of F, with diameters ρ_i≤δ, where δ≤1. Let A_i be the projection of V_i to R^n and B_i its projection to the last coordinate. For any t∈[0,1] belonging to B_i, the sets A_i for those indices i cover E: indeed, for every x∈E the point (x,t) lies in some V_i, and that index has t∈B_i and x∈A_i. Also diam(A_i)≤ρ_i. Enclose each B_i in an open interval J_i of length at most ρ_i+η_i, where the positive errors η_i are chosen so that Σ_i η_i ρ_i^s is arbitrarily small (for ρ_i=0 use an arbitrarily small interval; the same limiting argument applies). For every t∈[0,1], the A_i with t∈J_i cover E, so their s-dimensional covering cost is at least the δ-scale Hausdorff content of E. Integrate this pointwise inequality over t∈[0,1]. The integral of the sum of the nonnegative indicator functions 1_{J_i}(t)ρ_i^s is the sum of their integrals (Tonelli for a nonnegative series), and each interval contributes at most (ρ_i+η_i)ρ_i^s. Therefore the δ-scale s-content of E is at most Σ_i ρ_i^(s+1)+Σ_i η_iρ_i^s. Since H^s(E)=∞, its δ-scale content is unbounded as δ decreases to zero; letting the errors tend to zero shows that no covers of F at arbitrarily small scales can have bounded (s+1)-power cost. Equivalently H^(s+1)(F)=∞, so dim_H(F)≥s+1. Since this holds for every 0≤s<D, taking the supremum gives dim_H(F)≥D+1. (The use of Hausdorff contents in this argument is the standard covering characterization: infinite H^s(E) implies the δ-scale s-contents are unbounded as δ→0.)\n\nIt remains to handle D=0. Nonemptiness supplies x₀∈E. The map f:F→[0,1], f(x,t)=t, is 1-Lipschitz and onto, since (x₀,t)∈F for every t∈[0,1]. Lipschitz maps do not increase Hausdorff dimension, so dim_H([0,1])=1≤dim_H(F). This is the required lower bound D+1 in this case. The argument includes n=0: R^0 is a singleton, and the same projection argument gives dimension one for its product with the interval. Combining the lower and upper bounds proves the equality.",
  "cited_results":[
    {"result":"Hausdorff dimension threshold characterization: if s<dim_H(A), then H^s(A)=∞; if dim_H(A)<q, then H^q(A)=0; equivalently dimension is the critical exponent of Hausdorff measure.","type":"standard external theorem / mathlib candidate","candidate":"MeasureTheory.hausdorffMeasure_of_lt_dimH; MeasureTheory.hausdorffMeasure_of_dimH_lt in Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/HausdorffDimension.lean (importing Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Measure.Hausdorff).","applicability_obligations":"Check the pinned declarations’ full signatures and the NNReal/ENNReal coercions; use the characterization for the Euclidean metric and the specified definition of dim_H. The proof also uses the standard content-level consequence that H^s(E)=∞ forces the scale-s contents to be unbounded as the scale tends to zero; verify this directly from the Hausdorff-measure definition if not available as a named lemma."},
    {"result":"Lipschitz images do not increase Hausdorff dimension.","type":"standard external theorem / mathlib candidate","candidate":"LipschitzWith.dimH_image_le in Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/HausdorffDimension.lean.","applicability_obligations":"Apply to the coordinate projection F→[0,1], verify it is 1-Lipschitz for the concatenated Euclidean metric, and use surjectivity from x₀∈E to identify its image with [0,1]."},
    {"result":"The Hausdorff dimension of the unit interval is 1; the Hausdorff dimension of a subset of finite-dimensional Euclidean space is finite.","type":"standard external Euclidean Hausdorff-dimension facts; exact source declaration not yet probed","candidate":"Inspect Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/HausdorffDimension.lean and Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Measure/Hausdorff.lean; potentially derive the interval fact using Real.dimH_of_nonempty_interior and the ambient finite-dimensional bound using the same Euclidean Hausdorff-measure API.","applicability_obligations":"Confirm exact theorem signatures and that the interval’s ambient space is R (finrank 1). Establish D<∞ for E⊆R^n (for n=0 also immediate)."},
    {"result":"Tonelli theorem for a countable sum of nonnegative measurable functions, and the elementary covering characterization/scale-content properties of Hausdorff measure.","type":"standard external measure-theoretic results / direct Hausdorff-definition arguments","candidate":"For library source investigation: Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/Bochner/Basic.lean or the pinned Tonelli/Fatou files; Hausdorff covering definition in Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Measure/Hausdorff.lean.","applicability_obligations":"The integrands are ρ_i^s times indicators of open intervals, hence nonnegative measurable. Check carefully the zero-diameter cases and pass to vanishing enlargement errors; the content inequality must hold for every t and then at arbitrarily small scale. These steps are supplied in the proof at the level of the standard Hausdorff-cover definition, but require full expansion in a later independent NL0."},
    {"result":"Euclidean product distance is the square-root sum of squared factor distances; therefore diam(U×J)≤√(diam(U)^2+diam(J)^2).","type":"elementary metric calculation","candidate":"No imported theorem intended; verify the product metric isometry with the concatenation identification if Lean later uses a product/max metric.","applicability_obligations":"The current proof uses only the frozen concatenated Euclidean metric and the displayed coordinate calculation, not an unproved identification with a max product metric."}
  ],
  "boundary_cases":["E is nonempty as frozen; this is essential with dim_H(∅)=0 since the claimed formula fails for the empty set.","If dim_H(E)=0, the lower bound uses the surjective coordinate projection and nonemptiness, rather than choosing s<dim_H(E).","n=0 is included: R^0 is a singleton, and the projection lower bound and upper-cover argument remain valid.","The boundedness needed for finiteness of dim_H(E) follows from compactness; in fact the covering arguments use boundedness rather than compactness.","The metric throughout is the induced Euclidean metric on R^(n+1), not an unverified max-product metric."],
  "unresolved_points":["This is a natural-language proof candidate, not an independently audited or Lean-checked proof. In particular the lower-bound content/integration argument needs an exact, careful treatment of arbitrary cover sets, zero diameters, and the limiting enlargement errors to ensure the stated inequality without an implicit measurability assumption on B_i.","Pinned mathlib source has not been read for the complete signatures of the candidate dimension/measure lemmas, interval dimension, or finite-dimensional ambient bound. The cited standard facts therefore require later source verification and exact application review.","The finite-δ content assertion in the lower bound should be made fully quantitative in the later NL0: for s<D, scale-s contents become arbitrarily large, while an assumed bounded (s+1)-cost for product covers would bound those contents after integration. This is the intended contradiction and is not a certificate from the schema checker.","Stein–Shakarchi primary text has not been inspected; no theorem or proof route from that text is asserted or cited. The presented route is independently developed from Hausdorff covers." ]
}"}
